::MCR_U06_L02_Quiz_Q01::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L02_Quiz_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.1; MLA.MCR.GM.3
Question:
A side length changes from 5 cm to 20 cm. What is the scale factor?
{
=A. 4#Correct. The scale factor is 20 divided by 5, which equals 4.
~B. 15#This subtracts the side lengths. Similarity uses a multiplier, not a difference.
~C. 25#This adds the side lengths. A scale factor compares matching sides by division.
~D. 100#This multiplies the two side lengths. Use new length divided by original length.
}


::MCR_U06_L02_Quiz_Q02::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L02_Quiz_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.1; MLA.MCR.GM.3
Question:
A 6-inch side is enlarged by a scale factor of 3. What is the new side length?
{
~A. 9 inches#This adds 3 instead of multiplying. A scale factor is a multiplier.
~B. 12 inches#This doubles instead of multiplying by 3.
=C. 18 inches#Correct. 6 x 3 \= 18 inches.
~D. 3 inches#This uses the scale factor as the answer instead of applying it.
}


::MCR_U06_L02_Quiz_Q03::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L02_Quiz_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.1; MLA.MCR.GM.3
Question:
Which value represents a reduction?
{
~A. scale factor 4#A scale factor greater than 1 enlarges the figure.
=B. scale factor 1/2#Correct. A scale factor between 0 and 1 reduces the figure.
~C. scale factor 2#A scale factor of 2 enlarges the figure.
~D. scale factor 10#A scale factor of 10 enlarges the figure.
}


::MCR_U06_L02_Quiz_Q04::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L02_Quiz_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.1; MLA.MCR.GM.3
Question:
On a scale drawing, 2 inches represents 14 feet. How many feet does 5 inches represent?
{
~A. 21 feet#This adds 7 only once. Each inch represents 7 feet.
~B. 28 feet#This represents 4 inches, not 5 inches.
~C. 70 feet#This multiplies by 14 without first finding the feet per inch.
=D. 35 feet#Correct. 2 inches represents 14 feet, so 1 inch represents 7 feet and 5 inches represents 35 feet.
}


::MCR_U06_L02_Quiz_Q05::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L02_Quiz_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.1; MLA.MCR.GM.3
Question:
Which phrase best describes corresponding sides?
{
~A. The longest sides only#Corresponding sides are matching sides by position, not only the longest sides.
=B. Sides that match in the same position#Correct. Corresponding sides are compared to find scale factor.
~C. Any two sides in one figure#Sides must come from matching positions in similar figures.
~D. Sides that are always equal#Corresponding sides may not be equal; they are proportional.
}


::MCR_U06_L02_Quiz_Q06::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L02_Quiz_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.1; MLA.MCR.GM.3
Question:
A scale factor of 2.5 is applied to an 8-foot side. What is the new side length?
{
=A. 20 feet#Correct. 8 x 2.5 \= 20 feet.
~B. 10.5 feet#This adds 2.5 instead of multiplying by the scale factor.
~C. 16 feet#This uses a scale factor of 2 instead of 2.5.
~D. 5.5 feet#This subtracts 2.5, but a scale factor is a multiplier.
}


::MCR_U06_L02_Quiz_Q07::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L02_Quiz_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.1; MLA.MCR.GM.3
Question:
Two similar rectangles have matching sides 9 inches and 3 inches. If 9 inches is the new side and 3 inches is the original side, what is the scale factor?
{
~A. 6#This subtracts 3 from 9. Similarity uses division for scale factor.
~B. 12#This adds the side lengths. That does not give the multiplier.
~C. 27#This multiplies the side lengths. Scale factor compares them by division.
=D. 3#Correct. The scale factor is 9 divided by 3, which equals 3.
}


::MCR_U06_L02_Quiz_Q08::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L02_Quiz_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.1; MLA.MCR.GM.3
Question:
A 12-inch side is reduced by a scale factor of 1/3. What is the new side length?
{
~A. 36 inches#This multiplies by 3 instead of by 1/3. The figure should get smaller.
~B. 9 inches#This subtracts 3 instead of multiplying by 1/3.
=C. 4 inches#Correct. 12 x (1/3) \= 4 inches.
~D. 12 inches#This leaves the side unchanged. A reduction changes the length.
}


::MCR_U06_L02_Quiz_Q09::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L02_Quiz_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.1; MLA.MCR.GM.3
Question:
What stays the same in similar figures?
{
=A. shape#Correct. Similar figures keep the same shape while side lengths stay proportional.
~B. every side length#Side lengths may change when a figure is enlarged or reduced.
~C. area#Area usually changes when side lengths are scaled.
~D. perimeter#Perimeter can change when side lengths are scaled.
}


::MCR_U06_L02_Quiz_Q10::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L02_Quiz_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.1; MLA.MCR.GM.3
Question:
On a map, 1 inch represents 20 miles. How many miles are represented by 6 inches?
{
~A. 26 miles#This adds 20 and 6 instead of multiplying by the scale rate.
=B. 120 miles#Correct. 6 x 20 \= 120 miles.
~C. 60 miles#This uses 10 miles per inch, not 20 miles per inch.
~D. 3 miles#This divides instead of multiplying. More map inches represent more miles.
}


::MCR_U06_L02_Quiz_Q11::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L02_Quiz_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.1; MLA.MCR.GM.3
Question:
A rectangle has a side of 4 cm. A similar rectangle has the matching side of 10 cm. A different side of the first rectangle is 6 cm. What is the matching side in the second rectangle?
{
~A. 12 cm#This uses a scale factor of 2. The actual scale factor is 10 divided by 4.
~B. 13 cm#This adds 2.5 only partway instead of multiplying 6 by the scale factor. Use the same multiplier for matching sides.
=C. 15 cm#Correct. The scale factor is 10 divided by 4, then 6 x 2.5 \= 15 cm.
~D. 20 cm#This doubles 10 instead of applying the scale factor to the 6 cm side.
}


::MCR_U06_L02_Quiz_Q12::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L02_Quiz_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.1; MLA.MCR.GM.3
Question:
Which action describes a dilation?
{
=A. Enlarging or reducing a figure by a scale factor#Correct. A dilation changes size using a scale factor while keeping shape.
~B. Sliding a figure without changing size#This describes a translation, not a dilation.
~C. Turning a figure around a point#This describes a rotation, not a dilation.
~D. Flipping a figure over a line#This describes a reflection, not a dilation.
}


::MCR_U06_L02_Quiz_Q13::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L02_Quiz_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.1; MLA.MCR.GM.3
Question:
A scale model uses 1 inch for every 8 feet. How many feet are represented by 3 inches?
{
~A. 11 feet#This adds 8 and 3. A scale relationship uses multiplication.
~B. 16 feet#This represents 2 inches, not 3 inches.
~C. 64 feet#This squares 8 or uses the wrong multiplier.
=D. 24 feet#Correct. 3 x 8 \= 24 feet.
}


::MCR_U06_L02_Quiz_Q14::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L02_Quiz_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.1; MLA.MCR.GM.3
Question:
If a 10-inch side becomes a 5-inch side, what is the scale factor?
{
~A. 2#This compares original to new in the wrong direction for this question.
=B. 1/2#Correct. The scale factor is 5 divided by 10, which is 1/2.
~C. 5#This subtracts the side lengths. Similarity uses a ratio.
~D. 15#This adds the side lengths. Scale factor is not a sum.
}


::MCR_U06_L02_Quiz_Q15::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L02_Quiz_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.1; MLA.MCR.GM.3
Question:
A side length of 14 cm is enlarged by a scale factor of 2. What is the new length?
{
~A. 16 cm#This adds 2 instead of multiplying by 2.
~B. 7 cm#This divides by 2, which would reduce the length.
=C. 28 cm#Correct. 14 x 2 \= 28 cm.
~D. 12 cm#This subtracts 2 instead of applying the scale factor.
}


::MCR_U06_L02_Quiz_Q16::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L02_Quiz_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.1; MLA.MCR.GM.3
Question:
Which comparison should be used to find a scale factor from an original figure to a new figure?
{
~A. original length + new length#Adding side lengths does not give the multiplier.
~B. original length - new length#Subtracting gives a difference, not a scale factor.
~C. original length x new length#Multiplying side lengths does not compare the change.
=D. new length divided by original length#Correct. This comparison gives the multiplier from original to new.
}


::MCR_U06_L02_Quiz_Q17::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L02_Quiz_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.1; MLA.MCR.GM.3
Question:
A scale factor greater than 1 produces what kind of change?
{
=A. enlargement#Correct. A scale factor greater than 1 makes each side length larger.
~B. reduction#A reduction uses a scale factor between 0 and 1.
~C. no change#No change would use a scale factor of 1.
~D. reflection#A reflection flips a figure and is not described by scale factor size change.
}


::MCR_U06_L02_Quiz_Q18::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L02_Quiz_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.1; MLA.MCR.GM.3
Question:
On a blueprint, 4 inches represents 12 feet. How many feet does 1 inch represent?
{
~A. 4 feet#This repeats the drawing measurement. Divide 12 feet by 4 inches.
=B. 3 feet#Correct. 12 divided by 4 \= 3 feet per inch.
~C. 8 feet#This subtracts 4 from 12. Scale rate requires division.
~D. 48 feet#This multiplies 4 and 12. Find the value for one inch instead.
}


::MCR_U06_L02_Quiz_Q19::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L02_Quiz_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.1; MLA.MCR.GM.3
Question:
A 30-inch side is reduced by a scale factor of 1/5. What is the new side length?
{
~A. 150 inches#This multiplies by 5, which enlarges instead of reduces.
~B. 25 inches#This subtracts 5 instead of multiplying by 1/5.
~C. 30 inches#This leaves the side unchanged. A scale factor of 1/5 changes the length.
=D. 6 inches#Correct. 30 x (1/5) \= 6 inches.
}


::MCR_U06_L02_Quiz_Q20::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L02_Quiz_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.1; MLA.MCR.GM.3
Question:
Two figures are similar. One side in the first figure is 8 units, and the matching side in the second figure is 32 units. What is the scale factor from the first to the second?
{
~A. 24#This subtracts the side lengths. Similarity requires a multiplicative comparison.
~B. 40#This adds the side lengths. Scale factor is not a sum.
=C. 4#Correct. 32 divided by 8 \= 4.
~D. 256#This multiplies the side lengths. Use division to compare matching sides.
}

